---
export const prerender = true;

import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocCallout from "../../../components/docs/DocCallout.astro";
import DocCode from "../../../components/docs/DocCode.astro";
import DocRelated from "../../../components/docs/DocRelated.astro";

const toc = [
  { title: "What a mission is", href: "#what", depth: 2 },
  { title: "The shape", href: "#shape", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Features and dependencies", href: "#features", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Validation gates", href: "#validation", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Running one", href: "#running", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Watching progress", href: "#progress", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Intervening mid-run", href: "#intervene", depth: 2 },
  { title: "A complete mission", href: "#complete", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Failure modes", href: "#failure-modes", depth: 2 },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Orchestrating missions"
  subtitle="A mission is a dependency graph of features grouped into milestones — parallel where it can be, gated where it must be."
  description="Guide to nikcli missions: milestones, features, dependsOn graphs, validation gates, running and pausing, and intervening in a running mission."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="what" class="doc-section">
    <h2>What a mission is</h2>
    <p>
      A mission describes a body of work as <strong>milestones</strong>, each containing
      <strong>features</strong>. A feature names an objective and the agent that will do it; a
      feature can declare that it depends on others, which is what turns a list into a graph.
    </p>
    <p>
      The difference from a <a href="/docs/guides/loops">loop</a> is the shape, not the size. A loop
      is a pipeline that repeats on a cadence. A mission is a graph that runs once, to completion,
      with gates between milestones.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="shape" class="doc-section">
    <h2>The shape</h2>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`await client.mission.upsert({
  name: "search-v2",
  brief: "Replace the current search with a ranked index, without changing the public API.",
  milestones: [
    {
      id: "m1",
      name: "Indexing",
      validation: "scrutiny",
      features: [
        {
          id: "schema",
          name: "Index schema",
          objective: "Design the index schema and write the migration. No query changes yet.",
          agent: "plan",
        },
        {
          id: "writer",
          name: "Index writer",
          objective: "Write documents into the index on every mutation.",
          agent: "build",
          dependsOn: ["schema"],
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
})`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>brief</code> is the whole mission's context: every feature is executed with it in scope,
      so constraints that apply throughout — "without changing the public API" — belong there rather
      than repeated in each objective.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="features" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Features and dependencies</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>yes</td><td>What other features name in <code>dependsOn</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>yes</td><td>Human label in the UI and in exec history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>objective</code></td><td>yes</td><td>The prompt for this unit of work.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>agent</code></td><td>yes</td><td>Which agent runs it, and so which tools and permissions apply.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>model</code></td><td>no</td><td>Overrides the model for this feature.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tokenBudget</code></td><td>no</td><td>Caps this feature's spend.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dependsOn</code></td><td>no</td><td>Feature ids that must finish first.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td>no</td><td><code>pending</code>, <code>running</code>, <code>done</code>, <code>blocked</code>, <code>skipped</code>, <code>error</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Features with no unmet dependency are free to run together. Declaring a dependency you do not
      actually have is the most common way to make a mission needlessly serial.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Depend on outputs, not on order">
      <p>
        <code>dependsOn</code> should mean "this cannot be written until that exists", not "I would
        rather this happened first". Two features touching different files usually have no
        dependency at all, even when a human would do them in order.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="validation" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Validation gates</h2>
    <p>
      A milestone can declare how it is checked before the next one starts.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>scrutiny</code></td><td>The work is reviewed before the milestone is accepted.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>user-test</code></td><td>A human tests it; the mission waits.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>none</code></td><td>Roll straight into the next milestone.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="user-test blocks until someone answers">
      <p>
        A mission gated on <code>user-test</code> and left unattended is stopped, not failed. In
        unattended runs use <code>scrutiny</code>, and keep <code>user-test</code> for the
        milestones where a person really is going to look.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="running" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Running one</h2>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`await client.mission.start({ id: mission.id })
await client.mission.pause({ id: mission.id })
await client.mission.cancel({ id: mission.id })

const { data: list } = await client.mission.list()
const { data: one } = await client.mission.get({ id: mission.id })`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      As with loops, <code>generate</code> drafts a mission from prose and
      <code>templates</code> returns the built-in starting points.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const { data: draft } = await client.mission.generate({
  description: "Add OAuth login with Google and GitHub, behind a feature flag.",
})`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="progress" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Watching progress</h2>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const { data: execs } = await client.mission.execs({ id: mission.id })
const { data: recent } = await client.mission.recentExecs({ limit: 20 })`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      Feature <code>status</code> is the thing to render: <code>blocked</code> means a dependency has
      not finished, <code>skipped</code> means the mission moved past it, and <code>error</code>
      carries the reason in <code>error</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="intervene" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Intervening mid-run</h2>
    <p>
      A mission is editable while it runs. <code>mission.feature.mutate</code> changes one feature's
      status, records an error, or adds a dependency that was not obvious when the graph was
      written.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`// this turned out to need the schema after all
await client.mission.feature.mutate({
  id: mission.id,
  featureID: "writer",
  appendDependsOn: ["schema"],
})

// we handled this one by hand
await client.mission.feature.mutate({
  id: mission.id,
  featureID: "legacy-shim",
  status: "skipped",
})

// unblock a feature that failed for an external reason
await client.mission.feature.mutate({
  id: mission.id,
  featureID: "writer",
  status: "pending",
  error: undefined,
})`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="This is the escape hatch that makes missions usable">
      <p>
        Graphs written up front are wrong in small ways. Being able to add a dependency or skip a
        feature without tearing the mission down is what keeps a long-running one alive.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="complete" class="doc-section">
    <h2>A complete mission</h2>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="search-v2.ts">{`import { createNikcliClient } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk/httpapi"

const client = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:4096",
  directory: process.cwd(),
})

const { data: mission, error } = await client.mission.upsert({
  name: "search-v2",
  brief:
    "Replace the current substring search with a ranked index. " +
    "The public search API must not change shape; existing callers keep working.",
  milestones: [
    {
      id: "m1",
      name: "Indexing",
      validation: "scrutiny",
      features: [
        {
          id: "schema",
          name: "Index schema",
          objective: "Design the index schema and write the migration. Do not touch query code.",
          agent: "plan",
          tokenBudget: 40_000,
        },
        {
          id: "writer",
          name: "Index writer",
          objective: "Write documents into the index on every mutation, with tests.",
          agent: "build",
          dependsOn: ["schema"],
          tokenBudget: 120_000,
        },
        {
          id: "backfill",
          name: "Backfill",
          objective: "Backfill the index from existing rows. Idempotent and resumable.",
          agent: "build",
          dependsOn: ["schema"],
          tokenBudget: 80_000,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "m2",
      name: "Querying",
      validation: "user-test",
      features: [
        {
          id: "ranker",
          name: "Ranking",
          objective: "Rank results by relevance. Keep the response shape identical.",
          agent: "build",
          dependsOn: ["writer", "backfill"],
          tokenBudget: 120_000,
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
})
if (!mission) throw error

await client.mission.start({ id: mission.id })`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>writer</code> and <code>backfill</code> both depend only on <code>schema</code>, so they
      are free to run together once it lands; <code>ranker</code> waits for both. That is the whole
      point of writing it as a graph rather than a list.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="failure-modes" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Failure modes</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Everything runs one at a time</td><td>A <code>dependsOn</code> chain that encodes preference rather than necessity.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Feature stuck on <code>blocked</code></td><td>A dependency errored or was never started; check its <code>status</code> and <code>error</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mission stops and nothing happens</td><td>A <code>user-test</code> gate waiting on a human.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dependsOn</code> appears to be ignored</td><td>It names a feature <code>name</code> instead of its <code>id</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Features contradict each other</td><td>Shared constraints live in the objectives instead of the <code>brief</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>One feature consumes the whole budget</td><td>No <code>tokenBudget</code> on a broad objective.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <DocRelated
    links={[
      { title: "Missions", href: "/docs/missions", description: "The mission subsystem itself." },
      { title: "Automating with loops", href: "/docs/guides/loops", description: "The other shape: a pipeline on a cadence." },
      { title: "Missions API", href: "/docs/api/mission", description: "Every mission endpoint and payload." },
      { title: "Agents", href: "/docs/agents", description: "The agents features name." },
    ]}
  />
</DocsLayout>
